"# Chapter 14 — Composer introspection: widget round-trip is introspection too

A composer canvas is a live rendering of a Scheme form. Move a slider, the form updates. Change a value in the form, the slider follows. That two-way binding is a flavor of introspection you have not seen yet — the form is queryable from the widgets and the widgets are queryable from the form.

This chapter shows how the composer's round-trip is an introspection surface.

## 14.1 The composer canvas

A composer canvas binds to a target form:

```scheme
(composer/canvas :bind '(cart/main)
  (composer/slider :label \"BPM\" :bind '(cart/main :bpm) :min 60 :max 240 :step 1)
  (composer/piano-roll :bind '(cart/main :song) :range '(C3 . C6) :steps 32))
```

The canvas has one slider and one piano-roll. Both are bound to paths inside the target form.

The composer subsystem is live in this build: `composer/canvas`, `composer/slider`, `composer/piano-roll`, `composer/emit`, `composer/apply`, `composer/save`, and `composer/load` are real verbs — and the real way to introspect a canvas is to read the form it emits via `composer/emit`. Base Motoi exposes no dedicated widget-query verb (`composer/widgets`, `composer/widget-info`, `composer/set-widget-value` are not built-ins); the examples below introspect and mutate a canvas through the real `composer/emit` and `composer/apply` verbs instead. Because `type-of` reads a verb name (not a value), a small local `runtime-shape` helper classifies the record values for the shape examples.

```scheme
;; --- chapter setup: a live canvas + the modelled query verbs ---
(define nil '())
(define my-canvas
  (composer/canvas :bind '(cart/main)
    (composer/slider :label \"BPM\" :bind '(cart/main :bpm) :min 60 :max 240 :step 1)
    (composer/piano-roll :bind '(cart/main :song) :range '(C3 . C6) :steps 32)))
(define a-slider
  (composer/slider :label \"BPM\" :bind '(cart/main :bpm) :min 60 :max 240 :step 1))
;; runtime-shape: type-of reads a verb NAME; this local helper classifies the
;; canvas/widget record VALUES that composer verbs return.
(define (runtime-shape v) (cond ((pair? v) 'canvas-record) (else 'record)))
;; LLM stand-ins (see Chapter 13): modelled, since the model backend is none.
(define (rewrite form . opts) form)
(define (critique form) \"(modelled LLM critique)\")
(define (explain form) \"(modelled LLM prose)\")
(define new-form '(cart/main :bpm 180))
```

## 14.2 composer/emit — canvas to form

`composer/emit` walks the canvas and produces the current form.

```scheme
(composer/emit my-canvas)
;; => (cart/main :bpm 120 :song (sequence (note ...) ...))
```

The return is Scheme. Readable. Runnable. This is the canvas asked *what form do you produce right now?*.

## 14.3 composer/apply — form to canvas

`composer/apply` walks the canvas and updates each widget's state to match a form.

```scheme
(composer/apply my-canvas '(cart/main :bpm 180 :song (sequence (note :pitch 'C4))))
;; canvas widgets update: slider now shows 180, piano-roll shows one note
```

This is the canvas asked *update your widgets to match this form*.

## 14.4 The round-trip guarantee

`(composer/apply c (composer/emit c))` is identity. Emit the form. Apply the form. Everything ends up in the same state.

This guarantee is what makes the composer trustworthy. Save the emitted form to disk. Reload later. Apply. You are exactly where you left off. No lossy serialization. No hidden state.

## 14.5 Widgets are introspectable

Each widget carries metadata. Base Motoi has no dedicated widget-query verb; the real way to see a canvas's widgets is to read the form it emits and pick the widget sub-forms out of it:

```scheme
;; the emitted form is (composer/canvas (...) <widget> <widget> ...)
(define (canvas-widgets canvas)
  (filter (lambda (x) (and (pair? x) (symbol? (car x))))
          (cddr (composer/emit canvas))))

(map car (canvas-widgets my-canvas))
;; => (composer/slider composer/piano-roll)
```

Every widget's label, bind path, and config lives in that emitted form — read it and you have the whole canvas as data.

